+## The Option Help Bracket
+
+The bracket at the end of an option's help line, such as `[env var: NAME; default: 1; required]`, is built from items.
+{attr}`Option.help_extra_keys` lists their keys in the order they are shown. Each key has a method
+`get_help_extra_<key>(ctx)` that returns its value, or `None` to leave the item out, and
+{meth}`Option.format_help_extra_item` turns a value into the text in the bracket.
+
+A subclass adds an item by adding its key and writing its method. An item of a new key is shown as `<key>: <value>`:
+
+```python
+class UnitOption(click.Option):
+    help_extra_keys = (*click.Option.help_extra_keys, "unit")
+
+    def __init__(self, *args, unit=None, **kwargs):
+        super().__init__(*args, **kwargs)
+        self.unit = unit
+
+    def get_help_extra_unit(self, ctx):
+        return self.unit
+
+@click.command()
+@click.option("--timeout", cls=UnitOption, unit="s", default=30, show_default=True)
+def cli(timeout):
+    ...
+```
+
+The help line of `--timeout` ends with `[default: 30; unit: s]`. To change how an item is shown, override
+`get_help_extra_<key>` (its value) or `format_help_extra_item` (its text). {func}`click.formatting.append_help_extra`
+appends the bracket to a help text in the same form, for parameter classes of your own.
+
 ## Click's Wrapping Behavior
 
 Click's default wrapping ignores single new lines and rewraps the text based on the width of the terminal to a maximum of 80 characters by default, but this can be modified with {attr}`~Context.max_content_width`. In the example notice how the second grouping of three lines is rewrapped into a single paragraph.
 
 ```{eval-rst}
@@ -322,10 +352,49 @@ The default placeholder variable ([meta variable](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/
    The usage line follows a consistent convention: an element is optional when it is enclosed in square brackets and required when it appears outside them. A required argument appears as `FOO` and an optional one as `[FOO]`, with a variadic argument adding a trailing `...` (so `[FOO]...`). A single pair of brackets can group several tokens, so a chained group renders an extra command and its arguments as one optional, repeatable unit: `[COMMAND2 [ARGS]...]...`. A {class}`Group` that can run without naming a subcommand (`invoke_without_command=True`) also brackets the leading command, showing `[COMMAND]` instead of `COMMAND`. The `[OPTIONS]` placeholder is always bracketed, since adding options is itself optional.
 
    This mirrors the conventional utility-argument syntax for optional arguments and operands ([POSIX §12.1](https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9699919799/basedefs/V1_chap12.html), [`man-pages(7)`](https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man7/man-pages.7.html)), where a bracketed expression followed by `...` denotes zero or more occurrences. A subcommand name is a positional operand, so Click brackets it under the same rule.
 ```
 
+## Option Groups
+
+A command with many options is easier to read when related options are listed together. Give an option a
+`help_group`, and the help page lists it in a section of that name, after the `Options` section that holds the options
+without a group (`--help` among them):
+
+```{eval-rst}
+.. click:example::
+
+    @click.command(help_group_order=["Network", "Output"])
+    @click.option("--verbose", is_flag=True, help="Say more.")
+    @click.option("--color", is_flag=True, help="Use colors.", help_group="Output")
+    @click.option("--host", default="localhost", help="Server host.", help_group="Network")
+    @click.option("--port", type=int, default=80, help="Server port.", help_group="Network")
+    def serve(verbose, color, host, port):
+        pass
+
+.. click:run::
+
+    invoke(serve, args=["--help"])
+```
+
+The groups follow `help_group_order` of the command; groups that are not listed there follow in the order their first
+option appears. The second column starts at the same place in every section, so the page reads as one table.
+{meth}`Command.get_help_groups` returns the rows of each section, for a custom {meth}`Command.format_options`.
+
+The subcommands of a {class}`Group` can be grouped the same way: a command with `help_group="Admin"` is listed in an
+`Admin` section after the `Commands` section, and the group's `help_group_order` orders these sections too.
+
+```python
+@click.group(help_group_order=["Admin"])
+def cli():
+    pass
+
+@cli.command(help_group="Admin")
+def migrate():
+    """Migrate the database."""
+```
+
 ## Help Parameter Customization
 
 Help parameters are automatically added by Click for any command. The default is `--help` but can be overridden by the context setting {attr}`~Context.help_option_names`. Click resolves conflicts automatically: an option reusing one of the help option names takes it over, and the default help parameter is dropped once all its names are taken. A parameter merely named `help`, like `click.argument("help")`, does not disable it: both keep working independently.
 
 This example changes the default parameters to `-h` and `--help`
@@ -343,5 +412,65 @@ instead of just `--help`:
         pass
 
 .. click:run::
     invoke(cli, ['-h'])
 ```
+
+## Help Themes
+
+A {class}`HelpTheme` styles parts of the help page with {func}`style`: the section headings, the option, argument and
+command names in the first column, the `Usage:` prefix, and the bracket at the end of an option's help line. Set it with
+the `help_theme` context setting; subcommands inherit it from their group.
+
+```python
+THEME = click.HelpTheme(
+    heading={"fg": "yellow", "bold": True},
+    option={"fg": "cyan"},
+    usage={"bold": True},
+    extra={"dim": True},
+)
+
+@click.group(context_settings={"help_theme": THEME})
+def cli():
+    pass
+```
+
+Each field takes the keyword arguments of {func}`style`, or `None` to leave that part plain. Styles take no columns, so
+the page is laid out exactly as without a theme. As with any styled output, the codes are removed when the help is not
+written to a terminal, unless colors are forced with `color=True`. A custom {class}`HelpFormatter` gets the theme as its
+`theme` argument and as {attr}`HelpFormatter.theme`, and {meth}`HelpTheme.apply` styles a text as one of the parts.
+
+## Help Pages as Markdown
+
+{meth}`Command.get_help_markdown` returns the help page of a command as Markdown, for reference docs that stay in sync
+with the code. The command formats its help as usual, with a {class}`MarkdownHelpFormatter` instead of the terminal
+formatter, so every section of `--help` is there, in the same order, including sections that a custom
+{meth}`Command.format_help` writes.
+
+```python
+@click.command()
+@click.option("--count", default=1, show_default=True, help="Number of greetings.")
+@click.argument("name")
+def hello(count, name):
+    """Say hello."""
+
+with click.Context(hello, info_name="hello") as ctx:
+    print(hello.get_help_markdown(ctx))
+```
+
+```markdown
+# hello
+
+Usage: `hello [OPTIONS] NAME`
+
+Say hello.
+
+## Options
+
+| Name | Description | Details |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `--count INTEGER` | Number of greetings. | default: 1 |
+| `--help` | Show this message and exit. |  |
+```
+
+The `Details` column holds the items of the bracket at the end of the help line, as `--help` shows them. Style codes
+are removed, long texts are not wrapped, and `|` is escaped.
